---
/*
 * ConsentBanner — the site's cookie/analytics consent layer (P11 / D-82, CONS-01..07).
 *
 * ─── WHAT THE LAW ACTUALLY REQUIRES HERE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Art. 11.7a Telecommunicatiewet (the Dutch implementation of art. 5(3) ePrivacy) requires prior consent
 * for storing or reading information on a visitor's device, except where that is strictly necessary to
 * deliver the service the visitor asked for. Google Analytics is not in that exception, so nothing of it
 * may run — no script, no request, no cookie — until the visitor has actively said yes. Consent itself is
 * governed by the AVG: freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, by an affirmative act (art. 4(11)),
 * and as easy to withdraw as to give (art. 7(3)). The AP's 2025 enforcement round adds the practical test
 * this design is built against: REFUSING MUST BE AS EASY AS ACCEPTING, on the same layer, in the same
 * visual weight.
 *
 * ─── HOW THAT SHAPED THE COMPONENT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   · TWO IDENTICAL BUTTONS. "Alleen noodzakelijk" and "Analytics toestaan" are the same element, the same
 *     size, the same colour, the same border, one tab stop apart. There is no primary variant here on
 *     purpose: on this site `btn--primary` is a light-filled pill that would out-shout anything beside it,
 *     and using it for "accept" would be exactly the nudge the AP calls misleading.
 *   · NO "X". A close control that dismisses without a choice either records nothing (so the banner
 *     returns, which is worse) or is read as consent (which is unlawful). Both refusal and acceptance are
 *     one click; there is nothing to escape from.
 *   · NOT A MODAL. No scrim, no focus trap, no scroll lock — the site stays fully readable and operable
 *     behind it, which is what "no cookie wall" means in practice. Because it is not a dialog it is not
 *     announced as one: it is a labelled region, and `role="dialog"` is deliberately NOT used (that would
 *     promise modal semantics and focus management that do not exist and must not exist here).
 *   · EARLY IN THE DOM, LOW ON THE SCREEN. It sits directly after the skip link, so a screen-reader or
 *     keyboard visitor meets the choice near the start of the page rather than after every link on it.
 *     Visually it is a slim bar at the bottom edge, over the photography rather than across it.
 *   · RENDERED, NOT BUILT. The markup ships in the HTML with `hidden`; the script only removes the
 *     attribute. Nothing is constructed at runtime, so there is no layout shift, no flash for a visitor
 *     who already decided, and the banner cannot appear before its own styles.
 *
 * ─── NO-JS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Without JavaScript the banner stays hidden and the analytics tag is never loaded, so there is nothing to
 * consent to and nothing to ask. That is the honest baseline, not a degraded one.
 *
 * The element is always present (even when a decision exists) because it also carries `data-ga-id`, which
 * is how the runtime learns the measurement ID without hard-coding it in a second place.
 */
import { GA_MEASUREMENT_ID, CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY } from '../lib/legal';
---

<div id="lrf-consent" data-ga-id={GA_MEASUREMENT_ID} data-store-key={CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY}>
	<section class="consent" aria-labelledby="consent-title" data-consent-banner hidden>
		<div class="consent__inner">
			<div class="consent__copy">
				<h2 class="t-eyebrow t-eyebrow--accent consent__title" id="consent-title">Cookies</h2>
				<!-- THE FIRST LAYER IS SHORT ON PURPOSE — and it was measured, not guessed. The first draft ran
				     four sentences and took 327px, 51% of a 320×640 screen: informative, and much too close to
				     a wall. It must still carry everything an informed choice needs (what is optional, who
				     processes it, that it is reversible, where the detail is), so it was compressed rather than
				     trimmed of substance, and the detail moved one click away to /cookies/. -->
				<p class="consent__text">
					Deze website plaatst alleen wat nodig is om te werken. Met uw toestemming meet ik daarnaast
					bezoekstatistieken met Google Analytics. U kunt uw keuze altijd wijzigen.{' '}
					<a href="/cookies/">Wat wordt er opgeslagen?</a>
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="consent__actions">
				<button type="button" class="consent__btn" data-consent-decline>Alleen noodzakelijk</button>
				<button type="button" class="consent__btn" data-consent-accept>Analytics toestaan</button>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</div>

<script>
	/*
	 * The consent runtime. One bundled module, shared by every route, ~2 KB — deliberately not a consent
	 * management platform: the site has exactly one optional technology, and a CMP would be more code,
	 * more third-party requests and more data processing than the thing it governs (§15, blueprint 2.34.3).
	 *
	 * It owns three things and nothing else:
	 *   1. the stored decision (localStorage, the visitor's own choice — strictly necessary, so it needs no
	 *      consent of its own);
	 *   2. loading and configuring gtag.js, but only after an affirmative choice (Google's BASIC consent
	 *      mode: "Google tags blocked until consent is granted", no cookieless pings, no modelling — the
	 *      privacy-conservative option, chosen over Advanced deliberately);
	 *   3. telling the rest of the page what the current state is, via a `lrf:consent` event, so the
	 *      preference panel on /cookies/ never has to duplicate any of this.
	 */
	import { initConsent } from '../scripts/consent.js';
	initConsent();
</script>

<style>
	/* ============================================================= *
	 * The bar. Built from the site's own chrome material (the header/footer family), so it reads as part
	 * of the interface rather than as a third-party overlay. No gold fill — gold appears only as the
	 * hairline datum along the top edge and in the eyebrow, exactly as everywhere else (VIS-05).
	 * ============================================================= */
	.consent {
		position: fixed;
		left: 0;
		right: 0;
		bottom: 0;
		z-index: var(--z-overlay);
		background: var(--grad-chrome-footer);
		border-top: 1px solid transparent;
		/* The site's recurring champagne hairline as the bar's own top edge, painted over the border box so
		   it is one continuous line rather than a border colour approximating one. */
		background-image: var(--grad-datum), var(--grad-chrome-footer);
		background-size: 100% 1px, 100% 100%;
		background-position: 0 0, 0 0;
		background-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat;
		box-shadow: var(--shadow-overlay);
	}
	.consent[hidden] {
		display: none;
	}
	.consent__inner {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: var(--container-max);
		margin-inline: auto;
		padding: var(--space-lg) var(--gutter);
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: var(--space-md) var(--space-xl);
	}
	.consent__copy {
		flex: 1 1 26rem;
	}
	.consent__title {
		display: block;
		margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
	}
	.consent__text {
		font-size: var(--fs-body-sm);
		line-height: var(--lh-snug);
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
		max-width: 62ch;
	}
	.consent__text a {
		color: var(--color-interactive);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-interactive) 45%, transparent);
		text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
		transition: color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard),
			text-decoration-color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard);
	}
	.consent__text a:hover {
		color: var(--color-interactive-hover);
		text-decoration-color: currentColor;
	}

	/* THE TWO BUTTONS ARE ONE STYLE. Any difference between them — fill, size, order emphasis, colour — is
	   the thing the AP calls a misleading banner, so there is only one rule and both use it. */
	.consent__actions {
		flex: 0 1 auto;
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-sm);
	}
	.consent__btn {
		flex: 1 1 auto;
		/* 44px minimum target on touch (A11Y: pointer target size). */
		min-height: 2.875rem;
		padding: 0.6rem 1.4rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
		background: transparent;
		color: var(--color-text);
		font-size: var(--fs-body-sm);
		font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
		white-space: nowrap;
		transition: border-color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard),
			background-color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard);
	}
	.consent__btn:hover {
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
		background: rgba(var(--p-atmos-cool), 0.1);
	}

	/* Entrance: a short rise, and only where motion is welcome. Reduced-motion visitors get the bar in its
	   final position with no movement at all — a consent choice is not a place to spend a motion budget. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		.consent {
			animation: consent-rise var(--dur-component) var(--ease-standard) both;
		}
		@keyframes consent-rise {
			from {
				opacity: 0;
				transform: translateY(0.75rem);
			}
			to {
				opacity: 1;
				transform: none;
			}
		}
	}

	@media (max-width: 48rem) {
		.consent__inner {
			padding-block: var(--space-md);
			gap: var(--space-md);
		}
		.consent__actions {
			width: 100%;
		}
		/* THE TWO BUTTONS SHARE ONE ROW DOWN TO 320px. Stacked, they cost a whole extra button of height on
		   the smallest screens — and on a fixed bottom bar that is the difference between a strip and a wall.
		   `white-space: nowrap` was what forced the stack: it makes each button's min-content width its full
		   label, so the row could not fit two. Letting the label wrap instead costs one extra line of text
		   and saves ~50px of bar. `flex: 1 1 0` + `min-width: 0` keeps them exactly equal in width — which is
		   also the AP's equal-prominence requirement, now enforced by the layout rather than by the copy
		   happening to be the same length. */
		.consent__btn {
			flex: 1 1 0;
			min-width: 0;
			white-space: normal;
			line-height: var(--lh-snug);
			padding-inline: var(--space-sm);
		}
	}
	/* Small phones: the copy is the remaining height. One step down in size with the same leading keeps it
	   fully legible (14px is above the "tiny luxury" floor this system rejects) and buys back two lines. */
	@media (max-width: 26rem) {
		.consent__text {
			font-size: 0.875rem;
		}
	}
</style>
